    "turn_text": "Well, they're gimmicks, right? They're PR gimmicks. They're headlines, you know, that sort of thing. that what I advocate for is at the state level, we've been able to effectively prohibit it because we don't allow any funding to go towards inland infrastructure, which would bring, and there is no oil off the coast anyway, but in the event that there ever was, hundreds or thousands of years from now, but every year we do a provision within the budget that would prohibit that. And that was led by effort, was led by Senator Chip Campson. And so that's effectively been done. I've been arguing, much like Congressman Mark Sanford had this as part of his platform, is the issue of federalism. And we all know as conservatives that states do have rights. The federal government shouldn't be mandating everything to everyone across the country. Every region is different. And so if we were to allow federalism or states to have the opportunity to have the say to make that decision independent of the federal government, that is the kind of bill that can actually pass both chambers of Congress and be signed into law. It has much more potential if you allow states to have a say versus a federal mandate that you can't get Republicans on board with. So I approach things from a truly independent bipartisan way. And I know that most South Carolina's South Carolina voters, I believe that they agree",
