    "turn_idx": 145,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, something like that. We have very similar kind of stories. And when you have those setbacks are a couple of paths that you can take. And the principal of my high school happened to be a graduate of the Citadel, just like my father. And he said I could come back to school and I said no. He said well then you could take some night classes and sit for your GED and I heard test and that's like a four-letter word and I said hell no that's not happening. But and then he said well we could you know allow you to go to try and technical college in North Charleston take some college classes and get high school credit for those. So I actually started college when I was 17 in high school, finished my high school degree in six months and graduated, got my diploma a year early. And so now they call that dual enrollment, but back then nobody was doing that. And so I was one of the first students I believe in Berkeley County who was allowed to receive their high school diploma that way. And so that was in 1995 and then a year later the Citadel decided to allow women in.",
