    "turn_text": "here today. This is right up my alley. at any rate, but I wanted to follow my father's footsteps because I felt like I had failed him by dropping out of school, not being more confident and pushing through harder and staying in school and that sort of thing. I felt like a disappointment and a failure. And I didn't go there to be the first woman to graduate from there. That was not like my agenda. My agenda was to go to a place where I could be challenged, where I could face adversity and where I wouldn't give up on myself and I wouldn't quit. And so I learned a couple of things in that experience. One that I have no patience. I want it. I want it now. I'm going to work very hard to get it. But two other things is one, having courage taught me a lot about having courage and facing my fears, facing my failures. And having the courage to speak up, speak up for myself, speak up for others, giving a voice to someone who maybe would not otherwise have one. And then confidence was another one that I learned there to have confidence in myself. And if I was not confident in myself, nobody would be confident in me. And you can't lead an organization like you have your business. You can't lead an organization, a business, a school, or politically without having that sort of confidence so people can believe in you and what you want to do.",
