  {
    "episode_id": 97230,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/7/97230.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 275,
    "turn_text": "I love my children, but I do want them back in school. I think it's healthy. I do believe being in quarantine, being at home for so long, has been detrimental to our mental and physical health. And children are not, you know, I think early on we are worried about children passing it around and infecting each other, bringing in home and infecting other people. But what we are learning is that they are not the peace tree dishes that we thought that they were. They don't get as affected by it if they do get it. My child who had it had extremely mild symptoms, had a stuffy nose and was slightly tired for two days. But they are not sharing it, but it's actually what I've read is that it's difficult for them to share it or to give it to somebody else. So I'm assuming based on the timeline, like I gave it to my child. I mean, so it wasn't the other way around when we look at it. And so I do think that they need to be in school, but I do think that parents should have the option of homeschooling their kids if they're not comfortable with that. Parents should have the opportunity to make that decision for themselves as well if they're worried about it for",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
